course: books
lesson: 92
title: Just So Stories, The Crab that Played with the Sea, part 3: the Moon pulls the sea, and how tides really work
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Magician makes a deal: Pau Amma gets his hard shell back for eleven months of the year, but in the twelfth month it will go soft again, to keep him humble. Pau Amma thinks, then takes all the gifts. The Magician makes a Magic with all five fingers of his right hand, and Pau Amma shrinks into a little green crab beside the canoe, crying «Give me the scissors!»
explain: The girl gives him her scissors. He snaps them happily: now he can eat nuts, crack shells, dig holes, climb trees, breathe in dry air and hide under any stone. «I did not know I was so important. Kun?» «Payah kun,» says the Magician, laughing, and the tiny crab scuttles away.
explain: Now the Man must paddle all the way home. He grumbles; the Magician calls him lazy and says something unkind about his children, and the Man answers: then let the Sea work for him twice a day for ever. So the Fisherman of the Moon lets his line down and pulls the whole sea, and the canoe whizzes back into the Perak river.
explain: Ever since, says Kipling, the Moon has pulled the sea up and down and made the tides; when he pulls too hard we get spring tides, too softly, neap tides. Pau Amma's babies hide under stones, wave their scissors, climb palm-trees for coconuts and go soft once a year, and they nip you if you take them home in a pickle-bottle, which serves you right.
fact: The Magician: «I will give it back, Pau Amma, for eleven months of the year; but on the twelfth month of every year it shall grow soft again, to remind you and all your children that I can make magics, and to keep you humble.»
fact: Pau Amma: «I have made my choice. I will take all the gifts.» The Magician uses «all five fingers of his right hand», and Pau Amma becomes «a little green crab», so tiny that he could hide under the shadow of a dry leaf.
fact: The little crab: «I can eat nuts. I can crack shells. I can dig holes. I can climb trees. I can breathe in the dry air, and I can find a safe Pusat Tasek under every stone.»
fact: In the story the Magician calls the Man lazy and says his children will be the laziest people in the world, with a made-up name for the people of Malaya. That was an old, unkind idea about a whole people, and it is wrong: no people is lazy by nature.
fact: The Man: «If I am to be lazy all my days, let the Sea work for me twice a day for ever. That will save paddling.» The Rat of the Moon stops biting the line, and the Fisherman pulls the sea «past the Island of Bintang, past Singapore, past Malacca, past Selangor».
fact: The Magician warns the Fisherman to pull «twice a day and twice a night for ever ... But be careful not to do it too hard, or I shall make a magic on you as I did to Pau Amma.»
fact: Kipling: «From that day to this the Moon has always pulled the sea up and down and made what we call the tides»; too hard makes spring tides, too softly makes neap tides.
fact: Pau Amma's babies hate being «taken out of their little Pusat Taseks and brought home in pickle-bottles. That is why they nip you with their scissors, and it serves you right!» Kipling also says it isn't fair to hunt Pau Amma's babies just because of their father's old rudeness.
fact: The end-poem is a riddle about steamship companies of 1902: the lines that sail east past Singapore, like P. and O. and B.I., pass Pau Amma's playground; the Atlantic lines never go there.
fact: Real life: tides ARE made by the Moon, by its gravity, its pull, together with a smaller pull from the Sun; Kipling's fairy-tale reason has a true heart.
fact: Real life: most coasts get two high tides and two low tides in about 25 hours, so the tide comes about an hour later each day.
fact: Real life: spring tides, the biggest, come at full moon and new moon, when the Sun and Moon pull in a line; neap tides, the smallest, come at half moon. «Spring» here means «jump up», not the season.
fact: Real life: young crabs moult many times a year and older ones less often, some about once a year; the coconut crab of the Indian and Pacific Ocean islands really climbs palm-trees and cracks coconuts with its huge claws.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «yinz» (Pittsburgh) = you lot; «servo» (Australia) = petrol station; «innit» (London) = isn't it.
exercise: For how many months of the year does Pau Amma get his hard shell back? | Eleven | All twelve | Only six | Just one
exercise: What happens in the twelfth month? | His shell grows soft again | He grows as big as an island | He must go to the Moon | He loses his scissors
exercise: Why does the Magician make the shell go soft every year? | To keep Pau Amma humble | Because old shells wear out and crack in the salt water after eleven months | Because the Man asked him | To feed the sharks
exercise: What does Pau Amma choose in the end? | All the gifts | Only the shell | Only the scissors | Nothing at all
exercise: With which hand does the Magician make this second Magic? | All five fingers of his right hand | The little finger of his left hand | Both thumbs | No hands, only his breath
exercise: What does Pau Amma become? | A little green crab | A giant blue-green-black King Crab | A turtle | A fish
exercise: What does the little crab cry out for first? | «Give me the scissors!» | «Give me back my shell!» | «Take me home to Perak!» | «Make me big and strong again!»
exercise: Which of these is NOT in Pau Amma's list of «I can»? | I can fly | I can climb trees | I can crack shells | I can dig holes
exercise: How tiny is the new Pau Amma? | He could hide under the shadow of a dry leaf | He could sit in a teacup, but not in a thimble or a nutshell | He could hide in the girl's hair | He could hide behind the Moon
exercise: What does the Man complain about now? | Paddling all the way back to Perak | Losing his kris, his wavy dagger, in the deep water of Pusat Tasek | The Crab's scissors | The rain
exercise: What does the Man ask instead of being pulled home once? | That the Sea work for him twice a day for ever | That the little Crab carry the canoe home on his back, as a thank-you | That the Rat pull him | That the Magician fly him home
exercise: Who stops biting the line, and who pulls the sea? | The Rat stops; the Fisherman of the Moon pulls | The Fisherman stops; the Rat pulls | The Crab and the Man | The Magician and the girl
exercise: What does the Magician warn the Fisherman? | Not to pull too hard | Not to pull at night, when the Rat is awake and biting the line | To pull seven times a day | To go to sleep for a while
exercise: When the Fisherman pulls too hard, which tides do we get, says Kipling? | Spring tides | Neap tides | Low tides | No tides
exercise: When he pulls too softly? | Neap tides | Spring tides | Very high tides | Great floods
exercise: Where do Pau Amma's babies make their little Pusat Taseks? | Under stones and weed on the sands | Deep inside the coconuts that fall from the palm-trees on the beach | In the Moon | In the Man's house
exercise: What do some of Pau Amma's babies do on dry land? | Run up palm-trees and eat coconuts | Dig mountains as high as the Himalayas | Fly | Sing
exercise: Why, says Kipling, do the little crabs nip you? | They hate being taken home in pickle-bottles | They are always hungry, and your toes look like little fish | They want your shoes | They are cross with the Moon
exercise: What does Kipling say it isn't fair to do? | Hunt Pau Amma's babies for his old rudeness | Eat coconuts that belong to the crabs, now that they have scissors to open them | Go to the beach | Look at the Moon
exercise: «Kun?» asks the little crab. «Payah kun,» says the Magician. What does this mean? | «Is this right?» «That is quite right.» | «Goodbye.» «Good night.» | «Help!» «No.» | «Who are you?» «The Magician.»
exercise: Looking back: in part 2, what were the three gifts offered? | Safe holes, a land-and-sea home, and scissors | A canoe, a kris with a wavy blade, and the magic twin nut from the Wonderful Tree | Three shells | Food, water and fire
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Rat of the Moon always do? | Bite the Fisherman's line | Eat the Moon, a little every night, until it was thin like a fingernail | Pull the sea | Sleep
test: Noah: «the Moon pulls the tides cos a Fisherman's up there with a line, that's science innit.» (innit = isn't it) Put it right. | The Moon does cause tides, but by gravity, not a Fisherman | Right, there really is a Fisherman with a line | The tides come from a giant crab | The wind alone makes tides
test: The story calls the Man's children «the laziest people in the world». What should we think of that today? | It is an old, unkind idea and it is wrong | It is true about the people of Malaya, because the Magician said so and he is wise | It is a science fact | It is about crabs only
test: Why does the Magician refuse a shell for all twelve months? | He fears Pau Amma will grow bold and greedy again | He wants to keep the beautiful blue-green-black shell for himself, as a boat | He is cross with the girl | The shell is broken
test: What would happen if the Fisherman pulled the sea much too hard? | Huge tides, and trouble from the Magician | No tides at all, because the line would snap and the sea would stay still for ever | The Moon would fall | The crabs would fly
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Man asks for the Sea to do his work twice a day | Pau Amma refuses all the gifts | The girl keeps her scissors | The canoe sinks
test: Real life: what really makes the tides? | Mainly the Moon's pull, and the Sun's | A giant crab breathing in and out at the bottom of the sea, just as the story says | The wind | Whales
test: Real life: when do the biggest tides, spring tides, come? | At full moon and new moon, when Sun and Moon pull in a line | Only in the spring season | At half moon | Never
test: Real life: about how many high tides do most coasts get in about 25 hours? | Two | Only one | Seven | Twelve
test: Real life: what is the coconut crab famous for? | Climbing palm-trees and cracking coconuts | Living on the Moon with the Fisherman and the Rat, as the old stories tell | Flying between islands | Singing
test: Sam from Pittsburgh: «yinz should take crabs home in jars, they love it.» (yinz = you lot) What does Kipling say? | They hate it, and nip you | They love it, and wave their scissors to say thank you for the nice jar | They sleep in jars | They grow bigger in jars
test: Looking back: which earlier tale also ended with the hero getting a useful new body part, though he did not ask for it in that way? | The Elephant's Child, with his trunk | Taffy, with a new spear | The Stranger-man, with mud | The Tortoise, with a nut
test: Looking back: who in Kipling's book is called a «Tidy Pachyderm» who picked up melon rinds? | The Elephant's Child | Pau Amma | The Camel | The Rhinoceros
test: Looking back: in part 2, why couldn't soft Pau Amma leave Pusat Tasek? | Sharks and dogfish would eat him | He was too sleepy after playing with the sea all day and all night | He had no legs | The tree held him
test: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Man say the sea did to his canoe when it ran out? | Left only mud and upset it | Carried it away to the Moon, where the Rat bit the rope | Sank it | Painted it
test: Dara from Melbourne: «stopped at the servo, saw the tide out, it's the crab's fault.» (servo = petrol station) What is the real reason? | The Moon's pull moved the water away for a while | The crab went out to feed | The sea dried up | Someone drained it
test: ★★★ Pau Amma's shell is soft one month in twelve. If he lived ten years, for how many months was he soft in all? | Ten | Twelve | One hundred and twenty | Eleven
test: ★★★ Careful: the Man first says the sea comes «once a day and once a night»; the Magician tells the Fisherman to pull «twice a day and twice a night». Which is closer to real tides? | Twice a day and night in all: about two high tides in 25 hours | Twice a day AND twice a night: four high tides | Once a week | Seven times a day
test: ★★★ «Spring tide». Which word in English uses «spring» in the SAME sense as in «spring tide»? | «Spring up» (jump up) | «Spring» the season of the year | «Spring» in a mattress, a coil | «Spring» of fresh water
exam: Identify the arrangement concluded by the Eldest Magician in respect of Pau Amma's shell. | It is restored for eleven months, becoming soft in the twelfth | It is withheld for ever | It is restored for all twelve months | It is given to the girl-daughter
exam: Ascertain the agent who, according to the narrative, conveyed the canoe back into the Perak river. | The Fisherman of the Moon | The Rat of the Moon, having ceased to bite the line | Pau Amma, restored to his former size | The Man, paddling
exam: It is given that tides of exceptional height occur periodically. Select the response consistent with the established scientific facts. | Spring tides occur at new and full moon, when Sun and Moon act in line | Spring tides occur only in the spring season | Spring tides are caused by crabs | Spring tides occur at half moon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of laziness to an entire people in this tale? | It is an old and unkind prejudice, now recognised as wrong | It is an established fact | It is a matter of biology | It refers solely to crabs
